# Avatar and user badge

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

An avatar is a small picture that stands for a person. Home Assistant shows one in the sidebar for the signed-in user, in user lists and in the people settings. When a person has no picture, it shows their initials.

This page owns how the badge looks and what the Civica theme changes. What a shared screen may reveal about a person, and how to treat photos, is owned by [Presence, people and shared-screen privacy](../home-assistant/presence-and-privacy.html).

This page covers:

- Avatar and user badge
  - `ha-person-badge`
  - `ha-user-badge`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

The badge is a 40 pixel tile. It holds either the person’s picture or their initials on a light background. It has no name, status dot or border of its own. In the sidebar it is drawn at 32 pixels, with the person’s name beside it.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-ha-person-badge"><code>ha-person-badge</code></h3>

The person badge is used in the people settings. It looks the same as the user badge, described next, but takes a person instead of a user, and has two extra text size variables: `--person-badge-font-size` and `--ha-person-badge-font-size-long`.

<h3 id="child-ha-user-badge"><code>ha-user-badge</code></h3>

Shows a user. It finds the person whose account matches the user. If that person has a picture, the badge shows it, and follows the picture when it changes. If not, it shows the initials of the user’s name. If there is no user, it shows nothing.

It has one property, `user`, which Home Assistant sets in its own code. A dashboard author cannot add it to a card, and card-mod cannot reach it, because it is not inside a card. The sidebar and the user picker are where you see it.

What the theme controls:

- `ha-picture-border-radius`: the corner radius. Home Assistant draws a round badge by default. The Civica theme sets it to 0, so the badge is square.
- `light-primary-color`: the initials background. Civica sets #E8F1F8 in light mode and #D6E5F2 in dark mode.
- `text-light-primary-color`: the initials colour. Civica sets #0B0C0C in both light and dark mode.
- `ha-font-size-s`: the text size for three or more initials.

Calculated from these theme colours, the initials have a contrast ratio of 15.84 to 1 or more in light mode and 13.08 to 1 or more in dark mode, depending on the theme colour. With the Blue theme they are 17.13 to 1 in light mode and 15.25 to 1 in dark mode. Even so, the initials are only a hint, so keep the name visible.

The picture is drawn as a background, so it has no alternative text and no name that a screen reader can read. The initials are plain text inside the badge. Because Home Assistant gives no way to add a name to the picture, always put the person’s name beside it as text, as the sidebar does.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- The badge follows the person’s picture without extra setup.
- The initials give a fallback when there is no picture.
- The picture has no alternative text.
- A dashboard author cannot place the badge, resize it or add a name to it.
- The initials in dark mode are hard to read.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Show the person’s name as text beside every avatar. Never use the picture, the initials or a status dot alone to say who someone is or where they are. Say the state in words, such as "home" or "away". Keep the same place and size for avatars across a screen so people learn where to look.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.1.1 Non-text Content: the picture is decorative because the name is beside it.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: state is written, not shown by colour.
- 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum): see the calculated initials colours above.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the badge is not interactive.

Photos are personal data. Before you show one on a shared screen, read [Presence, people and shared-screen privacy](../home-assistant/presence-and-privacy.html). For pictures in general see [Pictures, rich content and embedded pages](../home-assistant/pictures-and-embedded-content.html).

